---
title: "You Only Need to Be Right 51% of the Time"
date: 2026-08-19
summary: "The greatest tennis player in history only won 54% of his points. Here is why the same rule applies to startups and software."
---

I've been playing and watching tennis for a long time now. There is something about the sport that just makes sense to me. It's incredibly fast, and once you step onto the court, it's entirely on you. There are no teammates to rely on and no one else to blame if things go wrong. It is a deeply mental game. Every single point is a strict reset.

If you know me, you probably know my favorite player is **Roger Federer**. For me, he represents the absolute peak of the sport. It wasn't just the fact that he won; it was how he won. He played with an unmatched class and elegance. Even in the most stressful, high-stakes moments, he made the hardest sport in the world look completely effortless.

Because he made it look so easy, we naturally think of him as this flawless legend who dominated every single second he played. But the reality is entirely different. During a recent commencement speech, he shared a statistic that completely shifted my perspective:

> In the 1,526 singles matches I played in my career, I won almost 80% of those matches. But what percentage of the points do you think I won in those matches? Only 54%.

That blew my mind. The player who defines perfection in tennis was losing almost half the time he swung his racket.

This got me thinking about how we view success, especially in the startup world. Whether I am making architectural decisions as the CTO at **Express My Health**, or writing software at **Lanaccess**, there is always this heavy pressure to be 100% right. We expect the perfect codebase, the flawless product design, and the perfect pitch to investors. We fear making the wrong move.

But the truth is, you don't need to be perfect. You only really need to be right about 51% of the time.

Success isn't about never making a mistake. It is about maintaining a slight edge. It's about making a decision, executing it, and seeing what happens. When you inevitably make a wrong move — and you will be wrong the other 49% of the time — you don't panic. You don't let it destroy your confidence. You use that 49% to learn, to debug the issue, to adjust the strategy, and to get better so you don't fall off.

In tennis, if you dwell on the point you just lost, you will lose the entire match. You have to let it go and play the next point.

Startups and software engineering are exactly the same. They are games of margins. You don't need to win every single point to succeed. You just need the optimism to handle the misses, the hard work to learn from them, and the focus to keep getting that 51% right.

— Jan